00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:32,140
And you stand like that.

188
00:14:32,140 --> 00:14:41,300
That's a lot of really, really practical teaching in Romans 2 and in the rest of the book of Romans.

189
00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:44,620
But I really want to focus on two verses here in the middle.

190
00:14:44,620 --> 00:14:53,300
And before we get there, have any of you ever been a part of an organization that was kind of restating their mission, vision, value statement?

191
00:14:53,960 --> 00:14:56,300
Or maybe there's a new organization being created?

192
00:14:56,300 --> 00:14:58,400
I mean, have you been a part of this process?

193
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:00,420
I've been a part of this process a lot.

194
00:15:00,420 --> 00:15:04,860
And I say this because Scriptures are always reimagining their mission, vision, and value statement.

195
00:15:04,860 --> 00:15:06,940
And I've thought about why that is.

196
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:17,040
And I think it's because Christians are stubborn and pastors always feel like they have to give new dialogue, new creative ways to convince people to do the thing that God's always called us to do.

197
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:22,640
Now, even if you look at Paul's epistles, a lot of the Paul's general epistles, they all say the same thing in a different way.

198
00:15:22,640 --> 00:15:28,640
Like, he's just trying to get the believers to do the thing that God's calling them to do.

199
00:15:29,460 --> 00:15:35,760
And so, when you think about mission, vision, and value statements, the mission is what do we want to see happen, right?

200
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:38,820
Like, what's the overarching big picture goal?

201
00:15:38,820 --> 00:15:43,720
I mean, the mission is how do we envision that happening in this specific context?

202
00:15:43,720 --> 00:15:49,060
And then the values are kind of working that out practically.

203
00:15:49,060 --> 00:15:56,560
If we want the vision to happen, what are the behaviors, what are the things that have to happen for our mission and our vision to be accomplished?

204
00:15:56,820 --> 00:16:04,340
And I think this is a helpful framework for our message this morning because I think Paul is going to do that in these two verses.

205
00:16:04,340 --> 00:16:06,360
He's going to give us, like, a big picture of things.

206
00:16:06,360 --> 00:16:10,420
This is what I want to happen in the church, and this is how I want it to happen.

207
00:16:10,420 --> 00:16:12,600
This is what I want to see it look like.

208
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:22,920
And then ultimately, he's going to do one specific behavior that is kind of the loop span or the catalyst for that mission, vision, and value.

209
00:16:22,920 --> 00:16:23,940
Sound like a plan?

210
00:16:23,940 --> 00:16:25,180
All right.

211
00:16:25,260 --> 00:16:29,680
I can't do people know that I often ask that question, and I don't care what the answer is because I already wrote the message.

212
00:16:29,680 --> 00:16:31,280
So that's what we're going to do.

213
00:16:31,280 --> 00:16:35,460
So Paul's mission for a unified church.

214
00:16:35,460 --> 00:16:40,000
He transitions, like I said, from theology to practice.

215
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:43,180
And then he gives it to this on using your gifts.

216
00:16:43,180 --> 00:16:45,960
Kent talked about that a few weeks ago, as he just said before.

217
00:16:45,960 --> 00:16:50,100
Understanding the gifts that you've been given, your calling, and living in light of that.

218
00:16:50,620 --> 00:16:55,000
And then he transitions to really practical before he reads in verse 9.

219
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,460
He says, don't just pretend to love others.

220
00:16:57,460 --> 00:16:59,120
Really love you.

221
00:16:59,120 --> 00:17:04,420
Hate what is wrong and hold tightly to what is good.

222
00:17:04,420 --> 00:17:08,780
And this is Paul's mission statement for the church at Rome.

223
00:17:08,780 --> 00:17:11,200
This is what he wants to be happening in their midst.

224
00:17:11,200 --> 00:17:14,160
He wants them to really love each other.

225
00:17:14,760 --> 00:17:18,180
You guys know, those of you who have been in church a long time, you know this is agape.

226
00:17:18,180 --> 00:17:19,660
This is unconditional love.

227
00:17:19,660 --> 00:17:22,860
He wants you to love each other the way that God has loved you.

228
00:17:22,860 --> 00:17:27,840
He wants you to hate sin, and he wants you to pursue righteous living.

229
00:17:28,200 --> 00:17:30,460
I don't think any of us would disagree with that.

230
00:17:30,460 --> 00:17:33,120
Like, that is a pretty good mission statement to the church.

231
00:17:33,120 --> 00:17:34,100
Love each other.

232
00:17:34,100 --> 00:17:35,920
Hate sin.

233
00:17:35,920 --> 00:17:40,660
Just like, pretty much, we could say amen and go home, right?

234
00:17:40,660 --> 00:17:43,160
That's Paul's mission statement.

235
00:17:43,160 --> 00:17:46,880
He says, we could love each other because God has loved us.

236
00:17:46,880 --> 00:17:48,040
1 John 4.19.

237
00:17:48,040 --> 00:17:50,140
We love because he first loved us.

238
00:17:50,140 --> 00:17:53,080
And then we define love.

239
00:17:53,080 --> 00:17:55,980
Our definition for that love is rooted in Jesus.

240
00:17:55,980 --> 00:17:57,320
1 John 3.16.

241
00:17:57,320 --> 00:18:00,580
This is how we know what love looks like.

242
00:18:00,580 --> 00:18:05,660
That he gave his life right so we should also give our lives for one another.

243
00:18:05,660 --> 00:18:10,120
So this is Paul's big picture mission statement.

244
00:18:10,120 --> 00:18:12,880
Understand how much God loves you.

245
00:18:12,880 --> 00:18:16,860
Extend that love to your brothers and sisters in Christ.

246
00:18:16,860 --> 00:18:20,560
Be obedient to what God commands you.

247
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:22,720
Anybody disagree with that?

248
00:18:23,900 --> 00:18:26,860
Not pretty solid advice.

249
00:18:26,860 --> 00:18:30,460
But it's somewhat abstract, right?

250
00:18:30,460 --> 00:18:36,040
It's so big picture that that's one of those things that as Christians we can just agree with and then leave.

251
00:18:36,040 --> 00:18:38,380
But what does it look like to live God?

252
00:18:38,380 --> 00:18:45,960
And so Paul transitions from his mission to the church to love each other, to take delight in doing good and hating evil.

253
00:18:46,200 --> 00:18:48,340
And then he gives a vision statement.

254
00:18:48,340 --> 00:18:52,240
What does he really want this to look like?

255
00:18:52,240 --> 00:18:54,240
And this is where it gets interesting.

256
00:18:54,240 --> 00:19:00,740
In Romans chapter 10, he says, love one another with genuine affection.

257
00:19:00,740 --> 00:19:05,300
Love one another with genuine affection.

258
00:19:05,440 --> 00:19:07,800
So he moves from his big picture command.

259
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:11,280
Essentially, he's saying, you should love each other because God says so.

260
00:19:11,280 --> 00:19:14,500
Like, you're commanded to love one another unconditionally.

261
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:17,760
But that's a big abstract idea.

262
00:19:17,760 --> 00:19:20,740
But what I really want you to do is absolutely like each other.

263
00:19:20,740 --> 00:19:24,240
I want you to become family with one another.

264
00:19:24,240 --> 00:19:25,960
He moves from agape to phileo, right?

265
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:30,320
Like, from commanded love, from the thing that you're supposed to do because God told you to do it,

266
00:19:30,320 --> 00:19:34,640
to absolutely liking each other, familial love.

267
00:19:34,640 --> 00:19:42,000
So Paul wants us, ultimately, as a church, he wants the church at Rome to move from loving each other because he has to,

268
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:47,480
to genuinely having familial affection for one another.

269
00:19:47,480 --> 00:19:51,760
Loving people that you don't like can be difficult and grimy.

270
00:19:51,760 --> 00:19:58,880
So if that was all Paul gave us, like, love each other because God loved you, hate sin, do what's right, and move on,

271
00:19:59,260 --> 00:20:04,860
we can all agree with that, but that gets really exhausting when you're constantly coming into a room full of people

272
00:20:04,860 --> 00:20:08,540
that you don't really like, that you have to love because God told you to.

273
00:20:08,540 --> 00:20:12,640
Sounds like church sometimes, right?

274
00:20:12,640 --> 00:20:19,260
And so Paul wants to know, like, this is, this is, yes, this is a big picture, this is what we need you to do,

275
00:20:19,260 --> 00:20:21,080
but I want it to look different.

276
00:20:21,080 --> 00:20:24,640
I want you to genuinely like each other.

277
00:20:24,640 --> 00:20:27,400
And this is difficulty.

278
00:20:28,200 --> 00:20:30,920
This takes time and then takes him in effort.

279
00:20:30,920 --> 00:20:36,140
It's my experience that there is no microwave for genuine affection.

280
00:20:36,140 --> 00:20:39,300
Like, it doesn't happen overnight.

281
00:20:39,300 --> 00:20:43,600
And so there's this placeholder called saying, like, just love each other because God said so.

282
00:20:43,600 --> 00:20:48,740
Like, do that for now, but I want you to move from that to something deeper, something better.

283
00:20:48,740 --> 00:20:50,100
I want you to become family.

284
00:20:52,600 --> 00:21:01,300
The body of Christ's church is supposed to be family, and then it takes time and energy and effort and prayer.

285
00:21:03,700 --> 00:21:07,280
The reality is a lot of us in this room, we love each other because God told us to.

286
00:21:07,280 --> 00:21:17,480
My heart for our church is that we would learn to live together in a way that moves from commanded love to genuine affection.

287
00:21:19,380 --> 00:21:20,000
So what is that?

288
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:21,420
How do we do that?

289
00:21:21,420 --> 00:21:26,420
How do we learn to love each other with genuine affection?

290
00:21:26,420 --> 00:21:30,880
How do we move from commanded love to genuine affection?

291
00:21:31,880 --> 00:21:43,480
See, Paul gives a behavior in action that I wouldn't even have on the top ten of my list if I were to make a list of how a church was supposed to move from commanded love to genuine affection.

292
00:21:43,480 --> 00:21:51,880
I think if we all sat down and made a list, some of our lists would be similar, and I doubt that any of you would include what Paul has first on his list.

293
00:21:51,880 --> 00:22:02,400
If he wants the church to move from commanded love to genuine affection, the first behavior that he gives, he says, take delight in honoring one another.

294
00:22:02,400 --> 00:22:04,960
Honor?

295
00:22:04,960 --> 00:22:07,340
That's kind of weird, right?

296
00:22:07,340 --> 00:22:13,260
Like, that's the first thing on the list if we're going to move from commanded love to genuine affection.

297
00:22:13,260 --> 00:22:15,860
How do we, what does that look like?

298
00:22:15,860 --> 00:22:21,640
In the rest of the book of Romans, there's going to be a ton of different behaviors of how to move from commanded love to genuine affection.

299
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:25,200
But the first one, the first one that he gives is omni.

300
00:22:25,200 --> 00:22:32,160
That doesn't necessarily hit our ears normal in our Western culture.

301
00:22:32,160 --> 00:22:35,160
Like, that's not a word that we use very often.

302
00:22:35,160 --> 00:22:38,340
When's the last time you've heard somebody use the word honor in front of it?

303
00:22:38,340 --> 00:22:41,680
Maybe, like, your kid's on the honor roll, possibly?

304
00:22:41,680 --> 00:22:44,420
But as of the matter, like, it's not a word that we use.

305
00:22:44,420 --> 00:22:46,060
It can be gay, and please wait.

306
00:22:46,060 --> 00:22:47,020
So what does it mean?

307
00:22:47,020 --> 00:22:53,760
If this is Paul's catalyst for moving from commanded love to genuine affection, we need to understand what it means.

308
00:22:53,760 --> 00:23:01,360
The word honor, it has this idea in the value of a product or the rank of an officer.

309
00:23:02,320 --> 00:23:04,680
The value of a product or the rank of an officer.

310
00:23:04,680 --> 00:23:08,600
So when you honor something, you're saying, this is how much you're worth.

311
00:23:08,600 --> 00:23:11,040
This is how much this product is worth.

312
00:23:11,040 --> 00:23:14,560
Or this is how much respect this person is worth.

313
00:23:14,560 --> 00:23:19,860
Those of you that were in the military know that as people go up and rank, you just command it to honor them more, right?

314
00:23:19,860 --> 00:23:21,220
To value them more.

315
00:23:21,220 --> 00:23:24,620
To go out of your way to show respect to them.

316
00:23:26,020 --> 00:23:32,140
Essentially, honoring one another is choosing to assign a high value to a servant.

317
00:23:32,140 --> 00:23:40,760
Now, now that we can't unpack that idea, we can see how this might be a catalyst for moving us from commanded love to genuine affection.

318
00:23:40,940 --> 00:23:47,940
If there's a room full of people who are going out of their way to choose to value the person next to them more than their service.

319
00:23:47,940 --> 00:24:00,020
To choose to say, you know what, I have a limited time, energy, and resources, but I am going to invest those time, energy, and resources into you, and into you, and into you, because you matter more than me.

320
00:24:00,020 --> 00:24:09,640
If we literally sacrifice and choose to see each other as more valuable than ourselves, imagine a room full of people all fighting to serve each other more.

321
00:24:09,640 --> 00:24:13,660
All fighting to sacrifice and sacrifice and love each other more.

322
00:24:13,660 --> 00:24:17,040
Imagine what happens as all of us do that together.

323
00:24:17,040 --> 00:24:24,880
Genuine affection begins to be formed because we're having conversations, we're sacrificing our time, our energy, and our resources.

324
00:24:24,880 --> 00:24:29,080
We're having each other into our homes, and we're doing life together.

325
00:24:30,020 --> 00:24:37,900
So Paul says, if you're going to move from commanded love to genuine affection, you've got to start to see each other as valuable.

326
00:24:37,900 --> 00:24:43,680
You've got to start to see each other as more valuable than we tend to see each other.

327
00:24:43,680 --> 00:24:46,260
How many yard saleers do I have in the middle of you?

328
00:24:46,260 --> 00:24:48,560
Many people that are up for yard sale.

329
00:24:48,560 --> 00:24:50,380
I grew up in a home with yard saleers.

330
00:24:50,380 --> 00:24:53,040
My mom is a big yard saleer.

331
00:24:53,040 --> 00:24:55,820
She's walking online, so mom, thanks for the illustration.

332
00:24:55,820 --> 00:24:59,940
There's a couple different types of yard saleers.

333
00:25:00,540 --> 00:25:02,240
Some yard saleers just want to get rid of stuff.

334
00:25:02,240 --> 00:25:07,360
So they just throw all this stuff out on the lawn, and they're like, just take it.

335
00:25:07,360 --> 00:25:10,180
25 cents, stickers on, everything, doesn't matter.

336
00:25:10,180 --> 00:25:17,080
And then, have you ever stopped at a yard sale where, like, the price tags on the junk is, like, ridiculous?

337
00:25:17,080 --> 00:25:20,000
These are the type of yard saleers that frustrated me.

338
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:24,700
Where all of their junk is worth, like, exceedingly, abundantly more than it was when it was new.

339
00:25:25,420 --> 00:25:27,380
There's no chance they actually want to get rid of it.

340
00:25:27,380 --> 00:25:35,620
And my theory behind this is, my theory is that the spouse of the person forced them to put their stuff out there, but they don't want to get rid of it.

341
00:25:35,620 --> 00:25:38,480
It's more like $25 for this thing, right?

342
00:25:39,760 --> 00:25:45,800
But, oftentimes, the value is in the eyes of the person that's putting the thing on it.

343
00:25:45,800 --> 00:25:53,020
They value this thing more than you might value it because it has context and understanding and a background behind it.

344
00:25:55,240 --> 00:25:57,820
This is the type of God that we have.

345
00:25:57,820 --> 00:26:04,300
The type of God that sees a high value in you when you don't see it in yourself.

346
00:26:04,300 --> 00:26:10,300
He sees a high value in your brother and sister in Christ when you are struggling to see it.

347
00:26:10,300 --> 00:26:12,340
When they are getting under your skin.

348
00:26:12,340 --> 00:26:13,740
When they are on your nerves.

349
00:26:13,740 --> 00:26:15,500
When they are stepping on your toes.

350
00:26:15,500 --> 00:26:18,020
When they don't seem valuable to you at all.

351
00:26:18,020 --> 00:26:20,620
God sees value in them.

352
00:26:22,240 --> 00:26:24,480
1 Corinthians 6, verse 20.

353
00:26:24,480 --> 00:26:26,740
You do not belong to yourself.

354
00:26:26,740 --> 00:26:29,700
For God bought you with a high price.

355
00:26:29,700 --> 00:26:31,580
Honor, value.

356
00:26:31,580 --> 00:26:35,740
God chooses to value you by buying you with a high price.

357
00:26:35,740 --> 00:26:40,340
When you weren't worth it, he chose to buy you with a high price.

358
00:26:40,340 --> 00:26:42,260
You must honor God with your body.

359
00:26:42,260 --> 00:26:44,260
1 Peter 1, 18-24.

360
00:26:44,260 --> 00:26:48,640
You know that God paid a ransom to save you from this empty life.

361
00:26:48,640 --> 00:26:51,220
And it was not paid with mere gold or silver.

362
00:26:51,480 --> 00:26:52,920
Very valuable things.

363
00:26:52,920 --> 00:26:56,520
Which means their value.

364
00:26:56,520 --> 00:26:59,640
It was the precious blood of Christ.

365
00:26:59,640 --> 00:27:03,120
The sinless, spotless reign of God.

366
00:27:03,120 --> 00:27:07,280
So we get good at honoring one another.

367
00:27:07,280 --> 00:27:11,160
We have to realize how valuable we are to the Creator of the universe.

368
00:27:12,100 --> 00:27:19,800
And as we learn to realize how valuable we are to the Creator of the universe, we lift our eyes up to our brothers and sisters in Christ.

369
00:27:19,800 --> 00:27:24,040
And we choose to assign to them the same value that God assigns to them.

370
00:27:24,040 --> 00:27:29,220
We have to realize that they are brothers and sisters created in the image of God.

371
00:27:29,220 --> 00:27:31,500
I love the phrase.

372
00:27:31,500 --> 00:27:33,540
I've heard many pastors say this before.

373
00:27:33,540 --> 00:27:36,180
For this very person, Jesus died.

374
00:27:36,180 --> 00:27:39,380
This is honor.

375
00:27:39,380 --> 00:27:43,580
When that person is doing something that you don't understand.

376
00:27:43,580 --> 00:27:49,920
When that person is leading in the way of your ministry because they want to do something a different way than you would want to do.

377
00:27:50,140 --> 00:27:56,940
What if you chose to say, for that very person, Jesus died.

378
00:27:56,940 --> 00:28:03,260
And if they are worthy of the blood of Jesus, they are worthy of my time and my energy and my resources.

379
00:28:05,340 --> 00:28:14,380
Paul said, if you want to move from commanded love to genuine, because he has to, to genuine affection, then it starts by seeing people exactly what it does.

380
00:28:14,380 --> 00:28:20,400
So that's by going out of your way to assign a high value to them.

381
00:28:20,400 --> 00:28:24,700
And that's something that we do with our time and with our energy and with our resources.

382
00:28:24,700 --> 00:28:32,900
But it's interesting to me that not only does he just say what we said on in one letter, it's framed in a competitive language.

383
00:28:33,500 --> 00:28:40,520
My translation doesn't really render it this way, but a lot of translations render it outdoing one another and showing on it.

384
00:28:40,520 --> 00:28:42,800
It's like this competitive thing.

385
00:28:42,800 --> 00:28:47,500
Like, again, imagine a room full of people who were all trying to beat each other at honoring one another.

386
00:28:47,500 --> 00:28:51,220
Imagine if we're all assigning more value to one another.

387
00:28:51,220 --> 00:28:54,000
It's going to breed genuine affection.

388
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:59,340
We're going to learn to love each other as we meet each other and see each other with value.

389
00:28:59,340 --> 00:29:01,560
I love that he frames me in competitive language.

390
00:29:02,240 --> 00:29:04,300
Maybe I'm the only one here.

391
00:29:04,300 --> 00:29:08,680
Does anybody ever read Paul and feel kind of offended?

392
00:29:08,680 --> 00:29:11,240
Like he's speaking to you like you're an idiot?

393
00:29:11,240 --> 00:29:11,680
Anybody?

394
00:29:11,680 --> 00:29:13,040
Okay.

395
00:29:13,040 --> 00:29:16,180
Sometimes that happens when you're my sister, Paul.

396
00:29:16,180 --> 00:29:21,020
And this is, I think, I don't know, I get a little vibe like he's singing us like we're toddlers.

397
00:29:22,740 --> 00:29:26,900
And he's like, he's going to convince us to value one another by making it competitive.

398
00:29:26,900 --> 00:29:32,760
Any of you ever tried to use this trick with toddlers when they're cleaning their room?

399
00:29:32,760 --> 00:29:36,900
I've done this before where it's like trying to get a kid to clean up after themselves.

400
00:29:36,900 --> 00:29:37,740
They're not doing it.

401
00:29:37,800 --> 00:29:41,800
And then he says, I bet I can clean this room faster than you can clean this room.

402
00:29:41,800 --> 00:29:43,680
Anybody ever done that before?

403
00:29:43,680 --> 00:29:45,120
What happens?

404
00:29:45,120 --> 00:29:48,840
Depending on the age, I was talking to Michelle about this this week.

405
00:29:48,840 --> 00:29:54,000
There's an age where they start to realize what you're doing and they're like, I bet you can't clean this room faster than I can.

406
00:29:54,660 --> 00:30:00,780
But there's like this sweet spot where they're like, no way, I'm going to beat you to clean the room.

407
00:30:00,780 --> 00:30:03,640
There's something about this competitive nature.

408
00:30:03,640 --> 00:30:12,040
It draws out some of those strengths that we have with MS, some of the ability that we have with MS that we don't even realize.

409
00:30:12,040 --> 00:30:13,020
And so it's all the same.

410
00:30:13,020 --> 00:30:16,260
Like there's one thing to just say, honor each other.

411
00:30:16,260 --> 00:30:19,480
He's saying, I bet you can't honor that person more than they honor you.

412
00:30:19,480 --> 00:30:23,560
And imagine, like, it seems silly, but imagine if you did that.

413
00:30:24,660 --> 00:30:29,920
Imagine if we were a room full of people that are like, I'm going to choose to honor you more than you choose to honor me.

414
00:30:29,920 --> 00:30:34,840
And we meet each other all at the bottom, serving one another sacrificially.

415
00:30:34,840 --> 00:30:40,280
Imagine a room full of people that get competitive about assigning a high value to one another.

416
00:30:40,280 --> 00:30:45,200
Or even better, assigning the same value that God assigns to them.

417
00:30:45,200 --> 00:30:50,540
Romans 5, 8, when we were utterly helpless.

418
00:30:51,540 --> 00:30:53,900
Christ came just at the right time.

419
00:30:53,900 --> 00:30:55,160
It's a perfect sense.

420
00:30:55,160 --> 00:31:04,040
So this means that when you would describe the person to your left or to your right as utterly helpless.

421
00:31:04,040 --> 00:31:06,060
Anybody?

422
00:31:06,060 --> 00:31:07,060
No?

423
00:31:07,060 --> 00:31:08,060
Am I going with you?

424
00:31:08,060 --> 00:31:13,720
Like, when you have these interactions with people in the church and you're like, I just don't.

425
00:31:13,880 --> 00:31:15,120
You can't, it's not today.

426
00:31:15,120 --> 00:31:23,760
It's in that moment that we remind ourselves that that's how we were before a holy devil.

427
00:31:23,760 --> 00:31:31,420
I know that we're in rebellion towards him.

428
00:31:31,420 --> 00:31:33,760
We're going the opposite way that he wants us to go.

429
00:31:33,760 --> 00:31:35,860
Doing the opposite things that he wants us to do.

430
00:31:35,860 --> 00:31:46,720
And it was in that moment where he told us to send his one and only son to purchase us with a high price, to choose to value us, to assign honor to us.

